## Project Overview
This project explores the study of traffic accidents that occurred in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia from 2016 to 2019[cite: 1]. The analysis is based on the dataset titled "Saudi Arabia Traffic Accidents and Casualties (Injured and Dead)"[cite: 1]. 

## Cases Analyzed
The project reviews and studies the following four main cases[cite: 1]:
* **Case 1:** Number of Accidents by region[cite: 1].
* **Case 2:** Comparison of Accidents, Deaths and Injuries by Year[cite: 1].
* **Case 3:** Number of Deaths by Region (2016–2019)[cite: 1].
* **Case 4:** The relationship between injury and death across all regions[cite: 1].

## Key Findings
* **By Region:** Makkah had the highest number of accidents throughout the entire period (2016–2019), while Al-Baha recorded the lowest[cite: 1]. 
* **By Year:** The year 2016 had the highest number of accidents compared to the other years, while 2019 had the lowest across all regions[cite: 1]. 
* **Severity:** Most accidents had no injuries or deaths in all the years[cite: 1].
* **Trend of Deaths:** The regression analysis shows that the number of deaths is decreasing in all regions over time[cite: 1].
* **Injuries vs. Deaths:** High injuries often, but not always, mean more deaths[cite: 1]. Outliers help identify regions where accident patterns are unusual[cite: 1].

## Conclusion
In summary, traffic accidents in Saudi Arabia have shown a notable decline in both frequency and severity from 2016 to 2019[cite: 1]. This positive trend suggests increased public awareness, better driving behavior, and possibly more effective enforcement of traffic laws[cite: 1].
